        [
            'id' => 'anorectal', 'title' => 'Piles, Fistula &amp; Fissure', 'icon' => 'fi-rs-bandage-wound',
            'items' => [
                ['q' => 'Do all piles need surgery?', 'a' => 'No. Early piles often improve with a high-fibre diet, fluids, medicines and avoiding straining. Surgery or laser treatment is advised for larger piles, or when bleeding, pain or prolapse keeps returning despite treatment.'],
                ['q' => 'Can an anal fistula heal without surgery?', 'a' => 'Rarely. Medicines may control infection for a while, but an established fistula tunnel usually needs a procedure to heal properly. The right technique depends on the position of the fistula.'],
                ['q' => 'Is bleeding from the anus always piles?', 'a' => 'No. Bleeding can also be caused by a fissure or, less commonly, by growths or cancer in the bowel. Please have any bleeding examined instead of treating it on your own.'],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'id' => 'scopy', 'title' => 'Endoscopy &amp; Colonoscopy', 'icon' => 'fi-rs-microscope',
            'items' => [
                ['q' => 'Is endoscopy or colonoscopy painful?', 'a' => 'The procedures are done under sedation, so most people feel little or no pain. You may feel mild bloating or throat soreness for a short while afterwards.'],
                ['q' => 'How do I prepare for a colonoscopy?', 'a' => 'The bowel must be cleared with a special diet and laxative solution the day before, so the view is clear. We will give you exact written instructions, and you should tell us about any regular medicines, especially blood thinners.'],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'id' => 'hysterectomy', 'title' => 'Hysterectomy', 'icon' => 'fi-rs-gynecology',
            'items' => [
                ['q' => 'Is hysterectomy the only treatment for my condition?', 'a' => 'Not always. Many conditions can first be treated with medicines or smaller procedures. Hysterectomy is usually considered when these have not worked, or when the condition is serious. Your surgeon will discuss every option with you.'],
                ['q' => 'Will I go into menopause after a hysterectomy?', 'a' => 'Removing the uterus stops your periods and the ability to become pregnant. Menopause happens right away only if both ovaries are also removed. If the ovaries are kept, they usually continue to work.'],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'id' => 'breast-thyroid', 'title' => 'Breast &amp; Thyroid Disorders', 'icon' => 'fi-rs-ribbon',
            'items' => [
                ['q' => 'Is every breast lump cancer?', 'a' => 'No. Most breast lumps are benign, such as cysts or fibroadenomas. However, no lump should be assumed harmless, so any new lump should be examined and, if needed, tested.'],
                ['q' => 'Will I need thyroid tablets after thyroid surgery?', 'a' => 'If the entire thyroid is removed you will need thyroid hormone tablets for life. After removal of only part of the gland, some people need them and some do not. Your surgeon will monitor your hormone levels and guide you.'],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'id' => 'cancers', 'title' => 'Stomach &amp; Intestine Cancers', 'icon' => 'fi-rs-stomach',
            'items' => [
                ['q' => 'What are the warning signs of stomach and intestine cancers?', 'a' => 'Persistent indigestion, unexplained weight loss, blood in stools or black stools, a lasting change in bowel habits, and anaemia without a clear cause. These have many causes, but if they last more than a couple of weeks, please get evaluated.'],
                ['q' => 'Is surgery the main treatment for these cancers?', 'a' => 'For many patients surgery is the main treatment, as removing the tumour offers the best chance of control. Depending on the stage, it may be combined with chemotherapy or radiation planned with oncology specialists.'],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'id' => 'ortho', 'title' => 'Orthopedic Care', 'icon' => 'fi-rs-bone',
            'items' => [
                ['q' => 'Do all fractures need surgery?', 'a' => 'No. Many fractures heal well in a cast or splint. Surgery is advised when the bone is badly displaced, the joint is involved, or the fracture is unstable.'],
                ['q' => 'Do I need surgery for joint pain?', 'a' => 'Usually not at first. Most joint pain is managed with medicines, exercises, physiotherapy, weight management and other non-surgical care. Surgery is considered only when these no longer control your symptoms.'],
            ],
